Sure, here is a code snippet that calculates the factorial of a given number using recursion in C++ with a time complexity of O(n):
```
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
using namespace std;
using namespace std::chrono;
int factorial(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}
int main() {
    int n;
    cout << "Enter a number: ";
    cin >> n;
    auto start = high_resolution_clock::now(); // start timer
    int result = factorial(n);
    auto stop = high_resolution_clock::now(); // stop timer
    auto duration = duration_cast<microseconds>(stop - start);
    cout << "Factorial of " << n << " is " << result << endl;
    cout << "Time taken by function: "
         << duration.count() << " microseconds" << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, the `factorial()` function takes an integer `n` as input and calculates its factorial using recursion. The base case of the recursion is when `n` is 0 or 1, in which case the function returns 1. Otherwise, the function multiplies `n` with the factorial of `n-1`.
The `main()` function prompts the user to enter a number, calls the `factorial()` function to calculate its factorial, and measures the time taken by the function using the `high_resolution_clock` class from the `<chrono>` library. The time complexity of this implementation is O(n), as the function calls itself n times to calculate the factorial of n.
To ensure that the function returns the value within a certain time limit, you can set a maximum time limit and check if the function has exceeded that limit using the `duration` variable. For example, you can add the following code after the function call:
```
if (duration.count() > MAX_TIME) {
    cout << "Function took too long to execute" << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
where `MAX_TIME` is the maximum time limit in microseconds. If the function takes longer than `MAX_TIME` to execute, the program will print a message and exit without returning the factorial value.

