Sure! Here's a Go code implementation that utilizes the divide and conquer algorithm to find the second largest number in an array:
```
package main
import (
	"fmt"
)
func findSecondLargest(arr []int) int {
	if len(arr) < 2 {
		return -1 // Not enough elements in the array
	}
	if len(arr) == 2 {
		if arr[0] > arr[1] {
			return arr[1]
		} else {
			return arr[0]
		}
	}
	mid := len(arr) / 2
	leftMax := findSecondLargest(arr[:mid])
	rightMax := findSecondLargest(arr[mid:])
	if leftMax > rightMax {
		return leftMax
	} else {
		return rightMax
	}
}
func main() {
	arr := []int{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9}
	secondLargest := findSecondLargest(arr)
	fmt.Println(secondLargest) // Output: 8
}
```
In this implementation, the `findSecondLargest` function takes an array of integers as input and recursively splits the array into two halves until the base case is reached (where there are only two elements in the array). The `mid` variable is used to split the array into two halves. The `leftMax` and `rightMax` variables are used to recursively find the maximum values from each half of the array. Finally, the function compares the `leftMax` and `rightMax` values to determine the second largest number in the array.
Note that the implementation returns -1 if there are not enough elements in the array. You can modify this behavior to suit your needs.

