int twist = rnd.Next(1,4);
switch (twist)
{
    case 1:
        Console.WriteLine("As they were waiting for the helicopter to arrive, they heard strange noises coming from the woods. Suddenly, a pack of wolves appeared out of nowhere, surrounding them.");
        break;
    case 2:
        Console.WriteLine("When they were finally airlifted to safety, they discovered that Jack had accidentally picked up a valuable artifact from the forest, and now they were being pursued by a group of ruthless treasure hunters.");
        break;
    case 3:
        Console.WriteLine("Emily and Jack were ecstatic to be rescued, but their joy was short-lived. It turned out that the helicopter that had picked them up was being flown by a notorious criminal, who was now holding them hostage.");
        break;
}
```
In the code above, we first create a new instance of the `Random` class to generate a random number between 1 and 3. We then use a `switch` statement to print out a different plot twist depending on the value of `twist`. You can copy and paste this code into your C# project to generate a random plot twist for the given short story.
